+# internal-api-client
+
+Thin, synchronous Python client for the internal reports/exports API (`/v2`).
+Requires Python 3.11+.
+
+```python
+from src import ApiClient, ClientConfig, ApiError
+
+client = ApiClient(ClientConfig.from_env(), transport)  # transport: see below
+reports = client.list_reports("globex", since=datetime(2024, 3, 1, tzinfo=timezone.utc))
+```
+
+## Setup
+
+### Configuration
+
+`ClientConfig` (`src/config.py`) is a frozen dataclass:
+
+| Field                   | Default                            | Meaning |
+|-------------------------|------------------------------------|---------|
+| `base_url`              | `https://api.internal.example.com` | API root; a trailing `/` is ignored |
+| `token`                 | `""`                               | Sent as `Authorization: Bearer <token>` |
+| `timeout_seconds`       | `10.0`                             | Passed to the transport on every send |
+| `retry_attempts`        | `5`                                | Total attempts for `list_reports` when it gets HTTP 400 |
+| `retry_backoff_seconds` | `0.5`                              | Base delay for the 400 retry (linear backoff) |
+| `rate_limit_retries`    | `3`                                | Extra sends after HTTP 429, per attempt |
+| `user_agent`            | `internal-api-client/0.4`          | `User-Agent` header |
+
+`ClientConfig.from_env()` reads only `INTERNAL_API_URL` and `INTERNAL_API_TOKEN`;
+every other field keeps its default. If `INTERNAL_API_TOKEN` is unset the token
+is empty and the API will answer 401.
+
+### Transport
+
+The client does no networking itself. It takes any object implementing
+`Transport.send(request: Request, timeout: float) -> Response`
+(`src/transport.py`). In production the host service injects the
+`platform-http` transport; tests inject an in-memory fake (`tests/fake_api.py`).
+
+`ApiClient` also accepts a `sleep` callable (default `time.sleep`) used for all
+retry waits, which lets tests run without real delays.
+
+## Endpoints
+
+| Method | HTTP request | Returns |
+|--------|--------------|---------|
+| `list_reports(account, since, until=None)` | `GET /v2/reports?account=&since=&until=` | `list[Report]` |
+| `get_report(report_id)` | `GET /v2/reports/{report_id}` | `Report` |
+| `list_exports(account, fmt="csv", created_after=None)` | `GET /v2/exports?account=&format=&created_after=` | `list[Export]` |
+| `get_export(export_id)` | `GET /v2/exports/{export_id}` | `Export` |
+| `search_accounts(query)` | `GET /v2/accounts/search?q=` | `list[Account]` |
+
+List endpoints read the `items` array from the JSON body. All methods are
+`GET`; there are no write operations.
+
+## How requests are built
+
+- **URL**: `base_url.rstrip("/") + path`, plus `?query` only if there are
+  parameters.
+- **Query parameters**: parameters whose value is `None` are omitted (e.g.
+  `until`, `created_after` when not given). Anything else, including an empty
+  string, is sent. Values are fully percent-encoded: only `A-Z a-z 0-9 - . _ ~`
+  are left literal, so `Smith & Co` becomes `Smith%20%26%20Co` and spaces are
+  `%20` (never `+`). Callers should pass plain, unencoded strings.
+- **Path ids**:
+  - `get_report` percent-encodes the id as a single path segment, including
+    `/` (`"r 1/x"` → `/v2/reports/r%201%2Fx`).
+  - `get_export` does **not** encode the id; it is inserted into the path
+    verbatim. Only pass ids that are already URL-safe (the ids the API itself
+    returns, like `e-1`, are). An id with spaces or reserved characters
+    produces an invalid request target, and depending on which edge node
+    handles the request you get a 400 or a 404.
+- **Headers** (identical on every request):
+  - `Accept: application/json`
+  - `Authorization: Bearer <config.token>`
+  - `User-Agent: <config.user_agent>`
+
+The client does no client-side validation of values such as `fmt`; the server
+rejects unsupported formats (currently `csv`, `xlsx`, `json` are accepted)
+with a 400.
+
+## Dates and times
+
+The two endpoints that take datetimes use **different conventions**. Pass
+timezone-aware `datetime` objects; the client converts them for you.
+**Naive datetimes are treated as UTC**, not local time.
+
+| Endpoint | Parameter(s) | Sent as | Example for `2024-03-05 15:00 UTC` |
+|----------|--------------|---------|------------------------------------|
+| `list_reports` | `since`, `until` | America/Bogota local time (fixed UTC-05:00, no DST), no offset suffix | `2024-03-05T10:00:00` |
+| `list_exports` | `created_after` | UTC with `Z` suffix | `2024-03-05T15:00:00Z` |
+
+Notes:
+
+- The reports backend has no timezone support and interprets timestamps as
+  Bogota local time (confirmed with the backend team), which is why reports
+  are converted rather than sent as UTC.
+- `list_reports`: `since` is required and **inclusive**; `until` is optional
+  and **exclusive**.
+- `list_exports`: `created_after` is optional and **exclusive** (strictly
+  after).
+- Seconds precision only; microseconds are dropped.
+- Timestamps in responses are returned as the raw strings from the server,
+  not parsed: `Report.period_start` is Bogota local time without offset (e.g.
+  `2024-03-02T09:00:00`), `Export.created_at` is UTC with `Z` (e.g.
+  `2024-03-02T10:00:00Z`). Parse them accordingly before comparing across
+  endpoints.
+
+## Errors
+
+- Any final response with status `>= 400` (after the retries below) raises
+  `src.models.ApiError`, with:
+  - `status`: the HTTP status code
+  - `path`: the endpoint path (e.g. `/v2/exports/e-1`), without base URL or
+    query string
+  - `body`: the raw response body as `bytes`
+  - `str(err)`: `"<path> returned HTTP <status>: <first 200 bytes of body>"`
+- Common statuses: `401` bad/missing token, `404` unknown id, `400` missing
+  or malformed parameters, `429` rate limited (only once retries are
+  exhausted).
+- Not wrapped in `ApiError` (propagate as whatever the underlying code
+  raises):
+  - exceptions raised by the transport (timeouts, connection errors);
+  - `json.JSONDecodeError` / `UnicodeDecodeError` if a success response body
+    is not valid JSON;
+  - `KeyError` / `ValueError` if a success response is missing expected fields.
+- Statuses below 400 (including any 3xx the transport doesn't follow) are
+  treated as success and parsed as JSON.
+
+## Retries
+
+There are two independent retry mechanisms. All waits go through the injected
+`sleep` callable.
+
+### Rate limiting (HTTP 429) — all endpoints
+
+When the gateway answers 429, the client sleeps and resends the identical
+request, up to `rate_limit_retries` (default 3) extra times, so at most 4
+sends by default.
+
+- Wait time is taken from the `Retry-After` header (header name matched
+  case-insensitively), interpreted as whole seconds.
+- If `Retry-After` is missing, empty, not an integer (including HTTP-date
+  form), or negative, the client waits 1 second.
+- If the last response is still 429, `ApiError(status=429)` is raised.
+- `rate_limit_retries=0` disables this.
+
+### Transient HTTP 400 — `list_reports` only
+
+The `/v2/reports` list endpoint has a history of returning spurious 400s
+(incident 2023-11), so `list_reports` retries on 400:
+
+- Up to `retry_attempts` (default 5) attempts **in total**.
+- Linear backoff: sleeps `retry_backoff_seconds * n` before the n-th resend —
+  0.5, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0 seconds with defaults.
+- Each attempt includes its own 429 handling, so in the worst case a single
+  `list_reports` call can make `retry_attempts * (rate_limit_retries + 1)` =
+  20 sends and block for a long time.
+- If every attempt returns 400, `ApiError(status=400)` is raised. Note this
+  means a `list_reports` call that the server genuinely rejects with 400 only
+  fails after the full backoff (~5 s by default).
+- Only the exact path `/v2/reports` is retried; `get_report`
+  (`/v2/reports/{id}`) is not.
+
+### Not retried
+
+Everything else: other 4xx, all 5xx (including 502/503/504), and transport
+exceptions such as timeouts. Callers that want to retry those must do so
+themselves.
+
+## Testing
+
+```
+python -m pytest
+```
+
+Tests run against `tests/fake_api.py`, an in-memory fake that mimics
+production routing: requests alternate between two edge nodes, one of which
+re-quotes the request target through a legacy proxy, and the origin rejects
+invalid RFC 3986 targets with 400. This is why encoding tests issue each
+request more than once.
